meta { title = "Installation", description = "Choose a native package and prepare your platform.", order = 0, group = "Using Kalandra" }

Choose the mixed CPU/Metal toolchain for a supported Mac when you need GPU execution. It also compiles ordinary CPU programs. Linux and Windows currently use native CPU packages. An editor-only package provides checking, formatting and the language server, rather than application compilation.

h2 { "Supported platforms" }

h3 { "macOS" }

Use macOS 26 or newer on Apple silicon. Native compilation requires the Apple SDK and system linker. Metal builds additionally require the pinned Apple Metal Toolchain component; the driver validates its signature and version. For GPU compilation, use Xcode 26.2 with its Metal Toolchain component, compiler version 32023.864. Open Xcode once to finish installing its components, select it as the active Command Line Tools in Xcode Settings → Locations, and install Metal Toolchain from Settings → Components. An arbitrary newer Metal compiler is not interchangeable with the pinned one. The exact requirements are recorded in the toolchain link below.

h3 { "Linux" }

Linux packages target x86-64 and ARM64 with an Ubuntu 24.04 / glibc 2.39 baseline. ARM64 support is available in builds after v0.99.0; select a release that lists the linux-arm64 asset. Standalone archives include private compiler dependencies while retaining their declared system-runtime requirements. Install g++, binutils and Git using your distribution package manager. Git is required for dependency management.

For Raspberry Pi, use a 64-bit OS on Pi 3, Pi 4, Pi 5 or Zero 2 W. Use Raspberry Pi OS Trixie (Debian 13); Bookworm has an older glibc than the packaged toolchain supports. Choose kalandra-native-cpu-standalone-linux-arm64.tar.gz. The target uses ordinary ARMv8-A CPU execution and does not provide VideoCore GPU acceleration. CI runs natively on ARM64 and checks the downloaded package in official Raspberry Pi OS userspace; physical Pi hardware, firmware and kernel variants are not qualified by that test. 32-bit OS installations and older 32-bit Pi models are unsupported.

h3 { "Windows" }

Use Windows 11 x86-64 with Microsoft C++ Build Tools and the Windows SDK. Install the Desktop development with C++ workload, including the MSVC compiler and Windows SDK. Use a Developer PowerShell or Developer Command Prompt for Visual Studio when compiling applications. The package supports native CPU execution.

h2 { "Install a native package" }

Download Kalandra from GitHub Releases. Open the release you want to install and choose the package for your operating system and architecture from its Assets section. Use the mixed macOS distribution, or the matching native CPU archive on Linux and Windows. For macOS CPU and GPU development, choose kalandra-native-metal-standalone.dmg. For x86-64, Linux and Windows use kalandra-native-cpu-standalone-linux-x86_64.tar.gz and kalandra-native-cpu-standalone-windows-x86_64.tar.gz respectively. ARM64 Linux builds use kalandra-native-cpu-standalone-linux-arm64.tar.gz, as described above. The standalone packages include private LLVM dependencies. Check the supplied SHA-256 sidecar before extracting. Keep the complete extracted toolchain directory together: its bin, lib and share resources are required.

link { url = "https://github.com/kalandra-lang/kalandra/releases", "Download Kalandra from GitHub Releases" }

On macOS, open the signed DMG and copy the complete toolchain directory to a permanent location before ejecting it. On Linux and Windows, extract the complete archive. Add the package bin directory to PATH. For example, if the complete package is at $HOME/kalandra, a POSIX shell can use:

codeblock { language = bash, """export PATH="$HOME/kalandra/bin:$PATH"
""" }

Use a permanent directory and add the equivalent path through your shell or Windows environment settings. Git is also required when installing source dependencies with kalandra install. Moving only the compiler executable loses its private resources. Standalone packages bundle the private LLVM toolchain; external-toolchain packages retain the requirements listed in their metadata.


link { url = "https://github.com/kalandra-lang/kalandra/blob/v0.99.0/native/metal-proof/toolchain.json", "Metal toolchain requirements" }

h2 { "Build from source" }

From a release-tag checkout, install CMake 3.28 or newer, Ninja, toml++ 3.4.0 and the pinned LLVM/MLIR/Clang 23.1.3 toolchain. Then use the native presets:

codeblock { language = bash, """cmake --preset native
cmake --build --preset native
ctest --preset native
""" }

The build-tree tools are under build/native/native. Metal configuration has additional opt-in dependencies and Apple toolchain requirements; follow the source guide rather than substituting an arbitrary compiler version.

link { url = "https://github.com/kalandra-lang/kalandra/blob/v0.99.0/native/README.md", "Native source-build instructions" }

h2 { "Verify installation" }

Run kalandra --version and confirm that it matches the release you downloaded. Each compiler, checker, formatter, parser and language-server executable reports its native toolchain version.

link { url = "/docs/getting-started", "Create, check and run the native starter" }

